1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"grade\":\"correct\",\"rationale\":\"The answer covers the central facts. It says Rule 6c-11 applies to open-end management company ETFs subject to conditions, and it correctly excludes UIT ETFs. It states that leveraged/inverse ETFs can rely on the rule, that their status alone does not require separate relief, and that derivatives use brings in Rule 18f-4 (derivatives risk program, VaR limits, reporting). It also says Rule 18f-4 neither grants nor bars Rule 6c-11 reliance, which matches the key point that these funds are not categorically excluded. It correctly says nontransparent/semi-transparent ETFs and share-class ETFs need separate exemptive relief. It names daily portfolio-holdings website disclosure as a condition, along with basket procedures and recordkeeping. The only gap is that master-feeder structures are not named among those needing separate orders. This is one example in a list and does not undermine the central framework. The added ETN point and the generic limited-user exception wording are accurate and introduce no material error.\"}","ttft_ms":4101,"type":"result","duration_ms":7144,"uuid":"6d0f3038-1941-4072-89e2-e83c1b1ed706","ttft_stream_ms":503,"time_to_request_ms":56,"first_content_frame_ms":503,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
